feat(hooks): rewrite the reference shim onto the three ingestion tools (#208)

RM-129. The shim was 807 lines across six harness events: git plumbing, a
`shasum` digest, a `$LOG/<run>/<digest>.json` layout, and a directory of marker
files whose line order two other scripts had to agree about. Every one of those
was work a second harness would have to reimplement identically, which is why
one harness got identity AND an activity record while every other harness got
identity and a blank log.

It now reads its own event, forwards it, and exits 0. `observe_session` (#207)
takes the whole identity lifecycle and the bookkeeping; `observe_tool_call`
(#206) takes the digest, the body and the `tool_call`; `describe_workspace`
(#205) is reached through observe_session, which composes it. The shim derives
no branch, folds no task, parses no remote, digests nothing, and knows nothing
about where a body is written.

Driven by OPS-019, OPS-020 and OPS-021, written first in
scripts/hooks/shim-selftest.sh and observed failing against the old shim: ten
failures naming register_agent where observe_session belonged, an absent
observe_tool_call, a local `shasum` and a local `worktree list`. The selftest
drives a synthetic payload for each of the six events against a stub that
records every tools/call, and its negative cases are the point — a shim that
still digested locally would pass every positive assertion.

Three things stay, deliberately, and the file says why at each one. The `git
commit` refusal must BLOCK the tool call, which only a harness can do; reading
this harness's event shape is the shim's whole job; and one remembered run id,
because a harness event carries a session id and never a run id, while the only
tool that maps one to the other also registers — and register_agent is metered
per caller (ADR-0025), so asking it per tool call would exhaust the meter.

Four things did NOT move and are named in the file rather than left to be
discovered: the 90-day retention sweep (whose writer is now the MCP, so the
sweep belongs there), `make innsegl-link`, the by-tree pointer the signer reads,
and the stop-time capture of ADR-0046. They are what remains to move.

The transport is one python3 client instead of curl, a dumped header file, four
`sed` expressions and a `printf`-built request — which also removes a real bug:
a branch name carrying a quote used to build a malformed call.

The frozen derivation moves to scripts/hooks/reference-derivation.sh, sourced
only under INNSEGL_HOOK_LIB and reachable from no event. MCP-043 compares the Go
port against the shell it was ported from, and a port whose only specification
is a deleted file is one nothing can catch drifting. The file says, at the top,
not to copy it into a shim.

One bug the live run found and a case now pins: `cmd && { ...; warn-if-detail }
|| warn-failed` returns the last conditional's status, so the failure arm fired
after a SUCCESSFUL stop — the hook printed "retired <run>" and "could not retire
<run>" one after the other. Both stop paths are if/else now.

Measured against the 807-line, 29-computation baseline: 653 lines total, 283 of
them code, and ten residual local computations, every one inside the four blocks
above. Shell-outs in the dispatch path, the reproducible proxy: 62 to 26.

Live, against the running stack: SessionStart, SubagentStart, six PostToolUse
calls, the PreToolUse refusal, SubagentStop and SessionEnd. The ledger shows
run_registered, six tool_call events at chain positions 17157-17162 and
run_retired; the activity log shows six entries, each with its body kept and
integrity "verified"; the dashboard renders "6 checked against the ledger, 0
altered, 0 past the window". Never asserted from memory.

parent_run_id is not carried by this path: observe_session takes no such
argument, so a subagent registered through it is a root run. Stated here rather
than left as a silent loss of ADR-0045's third member.

